    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Deep sea fishing from Tampa Bay</p>
      <h2>Offshore Fishing Past the Nine Mile Line</h2>
      <p>Our offshore trip is our deep sea fishing day. It starts at the nine mile line, where Florida's state waters end and federal water begins, and the boat holds a federal permit for reef and pelagic fish.</p>
      <p>Most of the day is spent on the bottom, dropping baits 40 to 100 feet or more onto Gulf wrecks, reefs and ledges. In September 2026, red grouper and red snapper led the offshore bite, with mangrove, lane and vermilion snapper mixed in.</p>
      <p>Capt. Ashmore runs our offshore trips. They go 6 or 8 hours, from $950 per boat, with room for up to six. If the Gulf won't cooperate, he calls it straight and you get a new date or your deposit back.</p>
      <div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-navy" href="/offshore-fishing/">See Offshore &amp; Deep Sea Trips</a> <a class="btn btn-outline" href="{{ site.booking }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reserve a Date</a></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Seasons &amp; species</p>
      <h2>What to Expect Fishing Tampa Bay</h2>
      <div class="seasons">
        <div class="season"><h3>Spring</h3><p>Kingfish move north past the mouth of the bay, with the run peaking in April and May. Snook open for harvest again in March and April.</p></div>
        <div class="season"><h3>Summer</h3><p>Tarpon roll through the bay in summer, and our tarpon trips run May through July. By late summer, redfish start pushing in from the surrounding waters and the sharks stay busy.</p></div>
        <div class="season"><h3>Fall</h3><p>Schools of mullet push along the shorelines on their way to the Gulf, and snook, redfish, tarpon and sharks follow them. Kings come back through in October and November.</p></div>
        <div class="season"><h3>Winter</h3><p>Sheepshead stack up on reefs, rock piles, docks and bridge pilings, and the bottom fishing stays steady nearshore. It's also when the crappie bite on the lakes.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <img src="/images/family-shark-bow.webp" alt="A family on the bow watching a shark come up to the boat" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="1040" class="first-img">
      <h3 class="h3-lg">Your first fishing charter</h3>
      <p>You don't need to know anything before you step on the boat. Your captain rigs the rods, picks the spot and talks you through the bite, the hookset and the fight. Kids are welcome, and inshore and crappie trips are the easiest place for young anglers to start.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


